Z||000000||fotofrank||06-12-99||07:53 PM||||OK, I put a tach from a 2.8 in my '84 2M4 ,changed the cap to .01mf and I belive it is not accuate. What other componants should be changed ? If anyone has a schematic for each tach I would like to compare them and make the necessary changes.
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||06-14-99||10:13 AM||||Which cap did you replace? I'm still trying to figure out how replacing a cap could change the read-out of the tachometer...<p>What RPM do you get when the car idles?
Z||000002||fotofrank||06-14-99||02:34 PM||||Clif,I replaced a .0082mf cap on the tach. I belive I read about this on this forum.I also swapped the circuit board from the 2m4 tach to the 2m6 tach.. My idle rpm, in nutral the tach indicates 1300 - 1400 RPM.I belive this indication is low.  How can a cap change a tach responce? If the cap is used as part of a filter or timing curcuit it can change the d.c. input of the tach by changing the friquency of the JK flip flop,or counter(same o same o).this JK or counter can then be rectifird to a calibrated d.c. or pulsed d.c. (no signosodal wave going positive and negitive but pulsed from 0 volts to 12 volts).The time between the pulses (longer or shorter)produce an evective d.c. level and a corrisponding direct curent.At least I think that is the answer.
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||06-14-99||04:39 PM||||Ok, that explains it a bit. Although I have disassembled a large amount of dashboards, I have never looked at the tach-circuitry in detail... I'll have a look tomorrow at the office and see what I can find. I still find it a bit strange they would use this method for a timing circuit because it would be very inaccurate... Cheap - yes, accurate - no.<p>The 8p2F cap that we were talking about somewhere on this Forum, was actually the cap value of the tachfilter, and not the tachometer itself.<p>You say it indicates 1300-1400 RPM when idling - that is way higher than it is supposed to be. Normal values for idling are 900-1000 RPM. But 1300-1400 RPM is about *exactly* what you would get from an unmodified V6 tachometer when used with the L4. So it looks like the new cap didn't make any difference.<br>
